  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAfter several days of dry conditions and above normal temperatures, \na change in the pattern is nearly upon us in the near term portion \nof the forecast period. However, we will squeeze out one more day of \nmuch above normal temperatures on gusty southerly winds today. After \ntoday, a cold front will sweep through the region and usher in \nnoticeably cooler temperatures along with increasing opportunities \nfor precipitation.\n\nThe upper flow pattern will feature southwest flow aloft across the \nNorthern Plains today through early next week as an upper trough \nworks across the Great Basin into the Rockies the next few days. \nThis disturbance is progged to push out into the Plains and Midwest \nearly next week as the pattern resets with a persistent upper trough \nacross the Western CONUS by mid to late next week.\n\nA tightening pressure gradient across the region will set up today \nas sfc high pressure drifts east across the Great Lakes and sfc low \npressure and associated frontal boundaries pull east of the Northern \nHigh Plains into the western Dakotas. A sfc warm front on \nconjunction with some weak mid level energy will track northeast \nacross SD today touching off some sprinkles and light rain across \nportions of northeast SD into west central MN. Southerly winds will \ngust mostly between 30-40 mph with a few locations perhaps topping \nout around 45 mph. 0.5 km wind progs set up a corridor of 30-45 knot \nwinds across our eastern zones during peak heating today. 925 mph \nthermal progs range between +15C to +25C with the warmest readings \nconcentrated across parts of central and south central SD. Thinking \nour western zones see more sunshine today versus our east. These \nwarm temperatures in combination with the strong winds and dry \nconditions in place will lead to elevated fire concerns across most \nof the forecast area. High to very high grassland fire danger will \nbe present today due to the mainly the strong winds. Min RH values \nbottom out between 30-40 percent this afternoon, so Red Flag \nconditions are not expected to develop.\n\nThe aforementioned low pressure system will track eastward tonight \nalong with an associated cold front. This front is progged to track \nwest to east across the forecast area late tonight through early \nSaturday morning. A few scattered light rain showers could accompany \nthis fropa but the more noticeable change with the increasing \nnorthwest wind driving in a much cooler air mass. Winds are progged \nto gust between 30-45 mph tonight through Saturday morning with the \nstrongest winds setting up across central SD. Probabilities of \nseeing wind gusts reach or exceed wind advisory criteria or 45 mph \nrange between 60-90 percent across portions of central SD and West \nRiver. Winds will begin to diminish as early as Saturday afternoon \nand sfc high pressure builds in. Saturday morning's cold air \nadvection pattern will drive a more seasonal air mass into the \nregion with daytime highs mainly in the 50s to low 60s through the \nweekend. Guidance progs another shot of mid level shortwave energy \nwill rotate into the region late this weekend into early next week \nas an upper trough works out of the Rockies into the \nNorthern/Central Plains. This disturbance is still expected to \ndeliver our next best widespread rain chances between 50-90 percent. \nCurrent probabilities of rainfall up to a half in or more range \nbetween 30-50 percent across our forecast area. The highest probs \nare concentrated across parts of south central SD northeast and east \ninto parts of the James and Big Sioux Valleys.",
